  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 247 PM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nDry weather conditions are expected through the middle of this week \ndue to upper ridging and surface high pressure in place. Current \nsatellite imagery depicts mid to high clouds pushing out of the \nregion as a subtle impulse aloft departs. Meanwhile, diurnal cumulus \nhas developed, mainly near or south of the I-70 corridor where \nconvective temperatures have been reached.\n\nUpper ridging building further into the Ohio Valley over the next \nfew days along with persistent southwest surface flow will promote \nincreasingly hot and humid conditions. Look for highs well in the \n90s for many locations with heat indices up to 105 possible each \nafternoon from Tuesday through Thursday. Little relief from the heat \nand humidity overnight will increase the threat for heat related \nillnesses. A Heat Advisory is now in effect from 2 PM EDT Tuesday \nthrough 8 PM EDT Thursday. Drink plenty of fluids, limit time \noutdoors during peak heating, and check up on relatives and \nneighbors.\n\nGuidance depicts the aforementioned upper ridge retrograding and \nthen amplifying across the central CONUS towards late week while \ntroughing develops over the Northeast. This will allow subtle waves \naloft to begin moving through the region within developing northwest \nflow. There is some slight uncertainty on Thursday regarding max \ntemperatures as the subtle wave approaches due to the potential for \nsome increase in clouds. Anomalous moisture in place along with weak \nforcing may also support the potential for isolated convection, but \nconfidence remains too low to include POPs at this time.\n\nFriday through Monday... \n\nConfidence in exact details decreases during the second half of the \nperiod due to diverging model solutions. Long range guidance \ngenerally depicts the upper ridge across the central CONUS \ncontinuing to amplify with persistent northwest flow across central \nIndiana. Multiple shortwaves aloft could move through during this \nperiod supporting the potential for isolated to scattered convection \nat times. In addition, a cold front may slowly push south late \nFriday through the weekend. This will provide additional forcing for \nconvection while also gradually cooling temperatures into Sunday. \nGreater cloud cover and low rain chances should help keep \ntemperatures cooler as well. Look for highs mostly in the 80s by \nSunday into early next week.",
